COPY public.sublevels (id, name, slug, sequence, raw_json, topic_id) FROM stdin;
1	Meaning of Userspace	meaning-of-userspace	1	{"topic_name": "Meaning of Userspace", "slug": "meaning-of-userspace", "levels": {"standard": {"explanation": "## Meaning of Userspace\\n\\n### Core idea\\nIn Linux, userspace is the execution area where ordinary programs run. A program in userspace may be a graphical application, a shell, a command-line utility, a daemon, a server, or a language runtime. Examples include bash, firefox, python, grep, sshd, nginx, and many desktop applications.\\n\\nUserspace is different from kernel space. Kernel space is where the Linux kernel performs privileged operating-system work such as process scheduling, memory management, filesystem access, networking, device control, and permission enforcement. A userspace program cannot directly control hardware or kernel memory. Instead, it requests services through controlled interfaces, usually by using library functions that eventually call system calls.\\n\\n### Operational meaning\\nWhen you open a terminal and run a command, that command normally runs in userspace. When you open a browser, the browser process also runs in userspace. When a service waits in the background, it can still be userspace software. Visibility is not the deciding factor. The deciding factor is whether the software is running as ordinary application-side code rather than privileged kernel code.\\n\\nYou can observe userspace processes with commands such as:\\n\\nbash\\nps -eo pid,user,comm\\n\\n\\nbash\\ntop\\n\\n\\nbash\\npgrep sshd\\n\\n\\nThese commands show running processes, most of which are userspace processes. The kernel itself is not listed as a normal user program because it is not just another ordinary process.\\n\\n### Why userspace matters\\nUserspace provides separation and protection. If a text editor crashes, it should not normally crash the whole operating system. If a browser wants to read a file, it must go through kernel-controlled permission checks. This boundary makes Linux safer, more stable, and easier to reason about.\\n\\nA useful summary is: userspace is where normal Linux software runs before it asks the kernel for protected system work.", "questions": [{"question": "Which statement best defines userspace in Linux?", "options": ["The area where ordinary application-side programs run", "The physical RAM reserved only for the CPU cache", "The firmware layer that starts before Linux boots", "The part of the disk used only for swap memory"], "answer": "The area where ordinary application-side programs run"}, {"question": "Which command can be used to view many running userspace processes?", "options": ["ps -eo pid,user,comm", "mkfs.ext4 /dev/sda", "shutdown now", "mount -o remount,ro /"], "answer": "ps -eo pid,user,comm"}, {"question": "Which of the following normally runs in userspace?", "options": ["bash", "Kernel scheduler core", "CPU interrupt controller hardware", "Kernel memory allocator internals"], "answer": "bash"}, {"question": "Why does a userspace program usually need the kernel to read a file?", "options": ["Because filesystem access is protected and mediated by the kernel", "Because files are stored inside the keyboard", "Because userspace programs cannot use memory", "Because the shell permanently owns all files"], "answer": "Because filesystem access is protected and mediated by the kernel"}, {"question": "What is the main difference between userspace and kernel space?", "options": ["Userspace is ordinary execution; kernel space is privileged operating-system execution", "Userspace is only for text files; kernel space is only for images", "Userspace exists only in desktop Linux; kernel space exists only in servers", "Userspace is hardware; kernel space is software"], "answer": "Userspace is ordinary execution; kernel space is privileged operating-system execution"}, {"question": "Which example best shows that userspace is not limited to visible applications?", "options": ["A background service such as sshd can run in userspace", "A monitor displays pixels", "A hard disk stores partitions", "A CPU has registers"], "answer": "A background service such as sshd can run in userspace"}, {"question": "What usually happens when a userspace program needs protected operating-system functionality?", "options": ["It requests the service through controlled interfaces such as system calls", "It directly rewrites the kernel image on disk", "It bypasses all permissions automatically", "It becomes firmware"], "answer": "It requests the service through controlled interfaces such as system calls"}, {"question": "Which command is most suitable for interactively observing active processes?", "options": ["top", "pwd", "echo hello", "mkdir test"], "answer": "top"}, {"question": "Which statement about userspace is technically accurate?", "options": ["Userspace can contain applications, shells, daemons, servers, libraries, and runtimes", "Userspace contains only the Linux kernel", "Userspace contains only the desktop wallpaper", "Userspace is another name for the BIOS setup screen"], "answer": "Userspace can contain applications, shells, daemons, servers, libraries, and runtimes"}, {"question": "What is a key benefit of separating userspace from kernel space?", "options": ["A fault in an ordinary application is less likely to damage the whole system", "All applications get unrestricted hardware access", "Linux no longer needs permissions", "Every process becomes a device driver"], "answer": "A fault in an ordinary application is less likely to damage the whole system"}]}, "layman": {"explanation": "## Meaning of Userspace\\n\\n### Simple explanation\\nThink of Linux as a large office building. Most workers do their jobs in normal office rooms. They type documents, answer calls, send messages, and use tools. This normal working area is like userspace.\\n\\nIn Linux, userspace is where normal programs work. A web browser, a terminal, a text editor, a music player, a command like grep, and a background service can all belong to userspace. Some of them have windows. Some of them run quietly without showing anything on the screen. Both types can still be userspace programs.\\n\\n### The control room idea\\nThe building also has a restricted control room. Only trusted staff can control electricity, lifts, water, security systems, and main switches. In Linux, this restricted control room is like the kernel. The kernel controls important things such as memory, files, hardware devices, network access, and process management.\\n\\nNormal office workers cannot directly touch the main electrical panel. They must request help from the control room. Similarly, normal Linux programs cannot directly control protected system parts. If an app wants to save a file, open a network connection, or print output, it asks the system through a proper path.\\n\\n### Why this is useful\\nThis design keeps the computer safer. If one normal program makes a mistake, the whole system should not collapse immediately. If a browser tab crashes, Linux tries to keep the rest of the computer working. If any program could directly control everything, one bug could damage the entire machine.\\n\\nSo, userspace does not mean weak or useless. Many powerful programs run there. It simply means they run in the normal program area, not in the restricted control area of the operating system.\\n\\nA good short definition is: userspace is the normal area where Linux programs run and ask the kernel for protected work when needed.", "questions": [{"question": "In the office-building analogy, what is userspace like?", "options": ["The normal working area where people do everyday jobs", "The locked electricity control room only", "The foundation stones under the building", "The parking area outside the building"], "answer": "The normal working area where people do everyday jobs"}, {"question": "In the same analogy, what is the kernel like?", "options": ["The restricted control room that manages important systems", "A worker's lunch box", "A visitor's chair", "A window curtain"], "answer": "The restricted control room that manages important systems"}, {"question": "Which of these can be part of userspace?", "options": ["A web browser", "A light bulb in the room", "A wooden table", "A power cable"], "answer": "A web browser"}, {"question": "Why should normal programs not directly control important system parts?", "options": ["A mistake could harm system safety or stability", "Programs are not allowed to show text", "Files do not exist in Linux", "The screen controls all permissions"], "answer": "A mistake could harm system safety or stability"}, {"question": "What should a normal app do when it needs protected work?", "options": ["Ask the system through a proper controlled path", "Take full control of the hardware by itself", "Delete the operating system", "Stop using memory forever"], "answer": "Ask the system through a proper controlled path"}, {"question": "Does userspace include only programs with visible windows?", "options": ["No, background services can also run in userspace", "Yes, only visible windows count", "Yes, only games count", "No, userspace contains only cables"], "answer": "No, background services can also run in userspace"}, {"question": "Which sentence gives a correct simple meaning of userspace?", "options": ["It is the normal area where Linux programs run", "It is the name of the computer monitor", "It is the place where only deleted files stay", "It is the hidden space inside a keyboard"], "answer": "It is the normal area where Linux programs run"}, {"question": "What might happen if one userspace app crashes?", "options": ["Usually Linux tries to keep the rest of the system working", "The computer must always physically break", "All files must become empty", "The keyboard must stop existing"], "answer": "Usually Linux tries to keep the rest of the system working"}, {"question": "Why is userspace not the same as being unimportant?", "options": ["Powerful programs such as browsers and servers can run there", "Only toy programs run there", "It contains no real software", "It is used only when the computer is off"], "answer": "Powerful programs such as browsers and servers can run there"}, {"question": "Which pair is the best simple comparison?", "options": ["Userspace is normal work area; kernel is restricted control area", "Userspace is the mouse; kernel is the mouse pad", "Userspace is a folder name; kernel is a file extension", "Userspace is the battery; kernel is the charger"], "answer": "Userspace is normal work area; kernel is restricted control area"}]}, "eli10": {"explanation": "## Meaning of Userspace\\n\\n### Very simple idea\\nImagine your computer is a school.\\n\\nThe apps are like students doing work in classrooms. A drawing app draws. A browser opens websites. A music app plays songs. A terminal runs typed instructions. These apps are working in a normal area. In Linux, this normal area is called userspace.\\n\\n### The teacher with keys\\nNow imagine there is a teacher who has keys to special rooms: the office, the science lab, the store room, and the computer room. Students cannot just run into these rooms and touch everything. They must ask the teacher.\\n\\nIn Linux, the teacher is like the kernel. The kernel protects important computer parts such as files, memory, network, printer, keyboard, and disk. Apps cannot just control all these important parts by themselves. They must ask in the correct way.\\n\\n### Why this helps\\nThis keeps the computer safer. If one student spills water, the whole school should not fall down. If one app gets stuck, the whole computer should not always stop.\\n\\nUserspace does not mean small or silly. Important apps can live there. It only means they are normal apps, not the special boss part of Linux.\\n\\nSo remember: userspace is the place where normal Linux apps work, and the kernel is the helper that protects important computer parts.", "questions": [{"question": "In the school example, what are apps like?", "options": ["Students working in classrooms", "Clouds in the sky", "Road signs outside school", "Shoes near the door"], "answer": "Students working in classrooms"}, {"question": "What is userspace like in the school example?", "options": ["The normal classroom area where students work", "A locked cupboard only", "A playground slide", "A school bus tire"], "answer": "The normal classroom area where students work"}, {"question": "Who is the kernel like in the school example?", "options": ["The teacher with keys to important rooms", "A student lunch plate", "A wall poster", "A football"], "answer": "The teacher with keys to important rooms"}, {"question": "Can normal apps safely touch every important computer part by themselves?", "options": ["No, they should ask the kernel", "Yes, always and without rules", "Only when it is raining", "Only if the app is red"], "answer": "No, they should ask the kernel"}, {"question": "Which one is a normal app that can work in userspace?", "options": ["A browser", "A school bell rope", "A pencil box", "A water bottle"], "answer": "A browser"}, {"question": "Why does Linux keep apps in a normal area?", "options": ["To help keep the computer safer", "To make all apps disappear", "To stop people from typing", "To turn the screen into paper"], "answer": "To help keep the computer safer"}, {"question": "If one app gets stuck, what should ideally happen?", "options": ["The whole computer should not always stop", "The computer must become a chair", "All other apps must become food", "The keyboard must fly away"], "answer": "The whole computer should not always stop"}, {"question": "What does the kernel protect?", "options": ["Important computer parts", "Only school bags", "Only cartoons", "Only pencil sharpeners"], "answer": "Important computer parts"}, {"question": "Does userspace mean the apps are silly or useless?", "options": ["No, important apps can live there", "Yes, all userspace apps are useless", "Yes, userspace means broken apps", "No, userspace means a lunch room"], "answer": "No, important apps can live there"}, {"question": "Which sentence is easiest and correct?", "options": ["Userspace is where normal Linux apps work", "Userspace is the computer's lunch", "Userspace is only the mouse wire", "Userspace is a broken window"], "answer": "Userspace is where normal Linux apps work"}]}}}	1
2	Ordinary Software World	ordinary-software-world	2	{"topic_name": "Ordinary Software World", "slug": "ordinary-software-world", "levels": {"standard": {"explanation": "## Ordinary Software World in Linux\\n\\n### Core idea\\nThe ordinary software world in Linux means the normal execution environment where day-to-day programs run. This is the world of userspace programs: shells, graphical applications, command-line tools, editors, servers, daemons, package tools, language runtimes, and desktop components. These programs are ordinary not because they are simple or weak, but because they run outside the privileged kernel execution area.\\n\\n### What belongs here\\nTypical ordinary Linux software includes:\\n\\n- Shells such as bash, zsh, and fish\\n- Commands such as ls, grep, cat, cp, and chmod\\n- Graphical applications such as browsers, file managers, and editors\\n- Background daemons such as sshd, cron, and NetworkManager\\n- Servers such as nginx, postgres, and redis\\n- Runtimes such as Python, Java, Node.js, and Ruby\\n\\nYou can observe this world by listing running processes:\\n\\nbash\\nps -eo pid,ppid,user,comm\\n\\n\\nYou can also inspect active processes interactively:\\n\\nbash\\ntop\\n\\n\\nOr search for a specific service:\\n\\nbash\\npgrep -a sshd\\n\\n\\n### Why it is called ordinary\\nOrdinary software runs with normal process privileges. It does not directly control CPU scheduling, kernel memory, device drivers, disk blocks, or network hardware. When it needs protected work, it asks the kernel through controlled interfaces. For example, a text editor may call library functions to save a file, and those library functions eventually request kernel help.\\n\\n### Important boundary\\nThis ordinary software world is not isolated from the operating system. It is connected to the kernel through system calls, libraries, permissions, files, sockets, and process management. The key point is that the access is controlled.\\n\\nA useful summary is: the ordinary software world is the complete userspace environment where normal Linux programs execute, cooperate, depend on libraries, and request protected work from the kernel when needed.", "questions": [{"question": "What does the ordinary software world refer to in Linux?", "options": ["The normal userspace environment where regular programs execute", "The internal firmware code stored in the motherboard", "The physical layer of the CPU cache", "The kernel-only area where device drivers are compiled"], "answer": "The normal userspace environment where regular programs execute"}, {"question": "Which command best helps you list many ordinary running Linux programs?", "options": ["ps -eo pid,ppid,user,comm", "mkfs.ext4 /dev/sda1", "reboot --force", "fdisk /dev/sda"], "answer": "ps -eo pid,ppid,user,comm"}, {"question": "Which item is part of the ordinary software world?", "options": ["The bash shell", "The kernel scheduler implementation", "A hardware interrupt line", "The CPU microcode update region"], "answer": "The bash shell"}, {"question": "Why can nginx be considered ordinary software even though it is a server?", "options": ["It runs as a userspace process that responds to requests", "It replaces the Linux kernel while running", "It directly controls the network card without the kernel", "It is stored only in BIOS memory"], "answer": "It runs as a userspace process that responds to requests"}, {"question": "Which command can search for a running sshd process with its command line?", "options": ["pgrep -a sshd", "chmod sshd", "umount sshd", "dmesg --erase sshd"], "answer": "pgrep -a sshd"}, {"question": "What makes ordinary software different from kernel-space code?", "options": ["Ordinary software runs with normal process privileges instead of full kernel privilege", "Ordinary software cannot use files at all", "Ordinary software exists only before boot", "Ordinary software must always have a graphical window"], "answer": "Ordinary software runs with normal process privileges instead of full kernel privilege"}, {"question": "Which group contains only ordinary userspace software examples?", "options": ["bash, grep, Firefox, nginx", "scheduler core, block driver, kernel memory allocator, VFS internals", "CPU registers, RAM chips, PCIe lanes, disk platters", "BIOS setup, boot ROM, firmware image, hardware clock"], "answer": "bash, grep, Firefox, nginx"}, {"question": "When ordinary software needs protected system work, what should it normally use?", "options": ["Controlled interfaces such as library calls and system calls", "Direct unchecked access to kernel memory", "Manual editing of CPU wiring", "A replacement for the bootloader"], "answer": "Controlled interfaces such as library calls and system calls"}, {"question": "Which statement about ordinary software in Linux is accurate?", "options": ["It may include visible apps, background services, command tools, and runtimes", "It includes only programs that show windows", "It includes only files inside /home", "It includes only commands typed by the root user"], "answer": "It may include visible apps, background services, command tools, and runtimes"}, {"question": "Why is the ordinary software world important for learning Linux?", "options": ["Most user-facing Linux activity begins there before kernel services are requested", "It removes the need to understand processes", "It proves that Linux has no background services", "It makes the kernel unnecessary"], "answer": "Most user-facing Linux activity begins there before kernel services are requested"}]}, "layman": {"explanation": "## Ordinary Software World\\n\\n### Simple meaning\\nThe ordinary software world in Linux is the normal place where programs do their work. Think of Linux as a large town. In that town, many people have different jobs. Some run shops, some deliver letters, some answer phones, some repair things, and some work quietly in offices. They are all doing normal town work.\\n\\nIn Linux, programs are like those workers. A browser opens websites. A terminal lets you type instructions. A file manager helps you open folders. A music player plays songs. A background service may quietly keep the network connected. A server may wait for people to send requests. These are different jobs, but they all belong to the ordinary software world.\\n\\n### Not weak or small\\nThe word ordinary does not mean useless. A server can be very important. A browser can be complex. A programming language like Python can run large applications. They are called ordinary because they are normal programs, not the deepest control part of Linux.\\n\\n### The control office\\nEvery town also has a control office. It manages important things like electricity, roads, water, and security. In Linux, the kernel is like that control office. Normal programs do not directly control everything. They ask the kernel when they need protected work, such as reading a file, saving data, using the network, or starting another program.\\n\\n### Why this matters\\nThis separation helps keep the system organized. If one normal program makes a mistake, Linux tries to prevent that mistake from damaging the whole computer. Also, when you learn Linux, it helps to see that browsers, shells, commands, daemons, and servers are not random separate ideas. They are different members of the same ordinary software world.\\n\\nA good simple definition is: the ordinary software world is the normal Linux program area where apps, commands, services, and servers run and ask the kernel for protected help when needed.", "questions": [{"question": "In the town analogy, what are Linux programs like?", "options": ["Workers doing different jobs in the town", "The stones under the road", "The weather above the town", "The color of the town gate"], "answer": "Workers doing different jobs in the town"}, {"question": "Which example belongs to the ordinary software world?", "options": ["A file manager used to browse folders", "A broken chair near a desk", "A wall clock inside a room", "A power plug on the floor"], "answer": "A file manager used to browse folders"}, {"question": "Why does ordinary not mean unimportant in this topic?", "options": ["Important programs such as servers and browsers can still be ordinary software", "Ordinary software is always a toy", "Ordinary software cannot do useful work", "Ordinary means the computer is switched off"], "answer": "Important programs such as servers and browsers can still be ordinary software"}, {"question": "What is the kernel like in the town analogy?", "options": ["The control office that manages important town systems", "A shop sign", "A bus ticket", "A flower pot"], "answer": "The control office that manages important town systems"}, {"question": "Which pair correctly belongs to the ordinary software world?", "options": ["Browser and terminal", "Keyboard wire and table leg", "Wall paint and window glass", "Fan blade and chair cushion"], "answer": "Browser and terminal"}, {"question": "What does a normal program do when it needs protected help?", "options": ["It asks the kernel through the proper path", "It takes over the whole computer directly", "It becomes a monitor screen", "It removes all files automatically"], "answer": "It asks the kernel through the proper path"}, {"question": "Why is the ordinary software world useful to understand?", "options": ["It helps connect apps, commands, services, and servers into one clear picture", "It shows that Linux has no services", "It proves that commands are not programs", "It means all programs must have windows"], "answer": "It helps connect apps, commands, services, and servers into one clear picture"}, {"question": "Which statement is correct?", "options": ["Some ordinary software is visible, and some runs quietly in the background", "Only visible programs are real Linux software", "Background services are always hardware parts", "Servers cannot be normal programs"], "answer": "Some ordinary software is visible, and some runs quietly in the background"}, {"question": "What is a background service in simple terms?", "options": ["A program that quietly keeps some job running", "A picture hidden behind the wallpaper", "A cable behind the computer", "A file that cannot be opened"], "answer": "A program that quietly keeps some job running"}, {"question": "Which simple definition is best?", "options": ["The ordinary software world is where normal Linux apps, commands, and services run", "The ordinary software world is only the mouse pointer", "The ordinary software world is the inside of a battery", "The ordinary software world is only deleted files"], "answer": "The ordinary software world is where normal Linux apps, commands, and services run"}]}, "eli10": {"explanation": "## Ordinary Software World\\n\\n### Imagine a school\\nThink of your computer like a school. Many people do different jobs there. Students read books. Teachers explain lessons. The librarian keeps books safe. The watchman opens the gate. The office staff keeps records.\\n\\nLinux also has many helpers. A browser helps you visit websites. A file app helps you open folders. A terminal helps you type instructions. A music app plays songs. Some helpers work quietly where you cannot see them, like someone ringing the school bell on time.\\n\\n### Normal helpers\\nThese helpers are the normal software workers of Linux. This normal place is the ordinary software world. It does not mean they are silly or weak. It only means they are normal helpers, not the main boss of the computer.\\n\\n### The main boss\\nIn the school, some things are special. Students should not open the principal's office, the electricity room, or the record room by themselves. They must ask someone responsible.\\n\\nIn Linux, the kernel is like that responsible boss. It protects important computer things. Normal apps ask for help when they need something important, like saving a file or using the internet.\\n\\n### Easy memory\\nThe ordinary software world is where normal Linux helpers do their jobs. Some are visible. Some are quiet. All of them help the computer do useful work.", "questions": [{"question": "In the school example, what are normal Linux programs like?", "options": ["Helpers doing different jobs", "Clouds above the school", "Stones on the road", "Leaves on a tree"], "answer": "Helpers doing different jobs"}, {"question": "Which one is a normal Linux helper?", "options": ["A browser", "A lunch spoon", "A football net", "A rain cloud"], "answer": "A browser"}, {"question": "What does the ordinary software world mean here?", "options": ["The place where normal Linux helpers work", "A playground outside the computer", "A broken school bench", "A box of crayons"], "answer": "The place where normal Linux helpers work"}, {"question": "Can some Linux helpers work quietly without being seen?", "options": ["Yes, some helpers work in the background", "No, every helper must show a big window", "Only if the computer is sleeping", "Only if the mouse is blue"], "answer": "Yes, some helpers work in the background"}, {"question": "What is the kernel like in the simple story?", "options": ["The responsible boss who protects important things", "A school lunch plate", "A pencil sharpener", "A playground ball"], "answer": "The responsible boss who protects important things"}, {"question": "Does ordinary software mean useless software?", "options": ["No, it can still do important work", "Yes, it can never help", "Yes, it is always broken", "No, it means a school bus"], "answer": "No, it can still do important work"}, {"question": "What should a normal app do when it needs something important?", "options": ["Ask the kernel for help", "Break the computer", "Hide under the keyboard", "Turn into a table"], "answer": "Ask the kernel for help"}, {"question": "Which app helps you open websites?", "options": ["A browser", "A notebook cover", "A water bottle", "A school bag"], "answer": "A browser"}, {"question": "Which sentence is correct?", "options": ["Normal Linux apps can be visible or quiet", "Normal Linux apps are always toys", "Normal Linux apps are only wires", "Normal Linux apps cannot do jobs"], "answer": "Normal Linux apps can be visible or quiet"}, {"question": "What is the easiest way to remember the idea?", "options": ["It is where normal Linux helpers do useful jobs", "It is where pencils sleep", "It is where clouds save files", "It is where chairs run websites"], "answer": "It is where normal Linux helpers do useful jobs"}]}}}	1
3	Where Apps Run	where-apps-run	3	{"topic_name": "Where Apps Run", "slug": "where-apps-run", "levels": {"standard": {"explanation": "## Where Apps Run in Linux\\n\\n### Core idea\\nIn Linux, applications run as processes in userspace. An app may be stored on disk as an executable file, script, package, or desktop launcher, but it does not truly run until the kernel creates a process for it and schedules that process on the CPU. The app's code executes in the normal, unprivileged execution area called userspace.\\n\\n### Stored location vs running location\\nDo not confuse where an app is stored with where it runs.\\n\\n- Executable files may be stored in places like /usr/bin, /bin, /opt, or a user's home directory.\\n- Shared libraries may be stored under paths such as /usr/lib or /lib.\\n- Runtime files, configuration files, and logs may live in other filesystem locations.\\n- The running app exists as one or more processes with memory, open files, permissions, environment variables, and process IDs.\\n\\nYou can inspect running applications with commands such as:\\n\\nbash\\nps -eo pid,ppid,user,comm\\n\\n\\nTo locate a running program by name:\\n\\nbash\\npgrep -a firefox\\n\\n\\nTo see the executable behind a process:\\n\\nbash\\nreadlink /proc/<PID>/exe\\n\\n\\n### What happens when an app runs\\nWhen you start an app, Linux creates a process, loads executable code and required libraries into virtual memory, assigns a PID, applies permissions, and gives it CPU time. The app runs in userspace, while the kernel manages privileged operations such as memory mapping, file access, device access, networking, and process scheduling.\\n\\nGUI apps, terminal commands, servers, and daemons all follow this basic idea. A browser window is userspace. A shell command is userspace. A background service is also usually userspace. Kernel threads are different: they are part of kernel-side execution and are not normal user applications.\\n\\n### Key point\\nApps are stored as files, launched into processes, executed in userspace, and supported by kernel services when protected operations are needed.", "questions": [{"question": "Where do normal Linux applications execute after they are launched?", "options": ["As userspace processes", "Inside BIOS setup only", "Directly inside the monitor hardware", "Only inside the bootloader"], "answer": "As userspace processes"}, {"question": "Which statement correctly separates stored apps from running apps?", "options": ["An app may be stored as a file, but it runs as a process", "An app is running only when its file is deleted", "An app runs only inside /etc", "An app cannot run if it has a filename"], "answer": "An app may be stored as a file, but it runs as a process"}, {"question": "Which command can list process IDs, parent process IDs, users, and command names?", "options": ["ps -eo pid,ppid,user,comm", "chmod -R 777 /", "mkfs.ext4 /dev/sda1", "shutdown -h now"], "answer": "ps -eo pid,ppid,user,comm"}, {"question": "What does the kernel create when an application is started?", "options": ["A process for the application", "A new physical CPU", "A permanent BIOS entry", "A new monitor display panel"], "answer": "A process for the application"}, {"question": "Which command can search for a running Firefox process with command-line details?", "options": ["pgrep -a firefox", "rm -rf firefox", "mount firefox", "fdisk firefox"], "answer": "pgrep -a firefox"}, {"question": "What does /proc//exe usually point to?", "options": ["The executable file used by that running process", "The physical keyboard driver chip", "The monitor brightness configuration", "The deleted files of that user"], "answer": "The executable file used by that running process"}, {"question": "Which item is usually not a normal userspace application process?", "options": ["A kernel thread", "A browser process", "A shell process", "A text editor process"], "answer": "A kernel thread"}, {"question": "What does Linux assign to identify a running application process?", "options": ["A PID", "A screen color", "A BIOS password", "A keyboard layout"], "answer": "A PID"}, {"question": "Why does a running app need the kernel even though it executes in userspace?", "options": ["The kernel provides protected services such as file access, memory management, and scheduling", "The kernel draws every letter of the app manually", "The kernel replaces the app's source code with firmware", "The kernel prevents all apps from using files"], "answer": "The kernel provides protected services such as file access, memory management, and scheduling"}, {"question": "Which statement about GUI apps, terminal commands, daemons, and servers is correct?", "options": ["They can all run as userspace processes", "They all run inside the BIOS", "They all become kernel threads automatically", "They all avoid process creation"], "answer": "They can all run as userspace processes"}]}, "layman": {"explanation": "## Where Apps Run\\n\\n### Simple idea\\nIn Linux, an app is first stored somewhere as a file. For example, a browser, text editor, terminal tool, or music player may be saved on the computer's storage. But being stored is not the same as running. A book on a shelf is not being read. A recipe in a notebook is not being cooked. In the same way, an app file sitting on disk is not yet doing work.\\n\\n### When the app starts\\nWhen you open an app, Linux turns it into a running activity called a process. A process means the app is now alive and doing work. Linux gives it an identity number, gives it memory to use, and allows it to take turns using the CPU. The CPU is the part that actually carries out instructions.\\n\\nSo the app is not running inside the icon, not inside the desktop shortcut, and not only inside the folder where the program file is kept. The app runs as a live process in the normal program area of Linux, called userspace.\\n\\n### Different apps, same idea\\nMany things follow this same pattern:\\n\\n- A browser runs as one or more processes.\\n- A terminal command runs as a process.\\n- A background service can run as a process even if you do not see a window.\\n- A server can run quietly and wait for requests.\\n\\nThe important point is that visibility is not the deciding factor. A visible app and a hidden background service can both be running programs.\\n\\n### Who controls important work\\nNormal apps do not directly control every important part of the computer. If an app wants to read a file, use the network, or save data, it asks Linux for help through a controlled path. This keeps the system safer and more organized.\\n\\nA simple definition is: apps are stored as files, but when opened, they run as live processes in the normal Linux program area.", "questions": [{"question": "What is the difference between an app stored on disk and an app running?", "options": ["A stored app is just saved; a running app is actively doing work", "A stored app is always faster than a running app", "A running app must be deleted first", "A stored app controls the CPU directly"], "answer": "A stored app is just saved; a running app is actively doing work"}, {"question": "In the book analogy, what is an app file like before it runs?", "options": ["A book sitting on a shelf", "A person already reading aloud", "A moving train", "A ringing school bell"], "answer": "A book sitting on a shelf"}, {"question": "What does Linux create when you open an app?", "options": ["A running process", "A new keyboard", "A new table", "A new power cable"], "answer": "A running process"}, {"question": "Does an app run inside its desktop icon?", "options": ["No, the icon only helps start the app", "Yes, the icon contains the running CPU", "Yes, the app runs only inside the image", "No, apps cannot run in Linux"], "answer": "No, the icon only helps start the app"}, {"question": "Which one can be a running process?", "options": ["A terminal command currently doing work", "A printed timetable", "A switched-off monitor", "A paper notebook"], "answer": "A terminal command currently doing work"}, {"question": "Can a background service be running even if you cannot see a window?", "options": ["Yes, some programs run quietly in the background", "No, every running program must show a large window", "Only if the monitor is turned off", "Only if the app has no name"], "answer": "Yes, some programs run quietly in the background"}, {"question": "What does Linux give a running app so it can be identified?", "options": ["An identity number", "A lunch plate", "A wall color", "A chair size"], "answer": "An identity number"}, {"question": "Why does a running app ask Linux for help when saving a file?", "options": ["Saving files is controlled by the system for safety", "Files are stored inside the mouse", "Apps are not allowed to use text", "Screens save files automatically"], "answer": "Saving files is controlled by the system for safety"}, {"question": "Which statement is correct about visible and hidden programs?", "options": ["Both visible apps and hidden services can be running programs", "Only hidden services are real programs", "Only visible windows can use memory", "Hidden services are always hardware"], "answer": "Both visible apps and hidden services can be running programs"}, {"question": "Which simple definition is best?", "options": ["Apps are stored as files but run as live processes", "Apps run only as pictures on the screen", "Apps run only inside the keyboard", "Apps are never stored on disk"], "answer": "Apps are stored as files but run as live processes"}]}, "eli10": {"explanation": "## Where Apps Run\\n\\n### Imagine a toy box\\nImagine you have a toy car in a toy box. When the toy car is inside the box, it is not moving. It is only stored there. When you take it out and push it, now it is doing something.\\n\\nAn app in Linux is similar. Before you open it, it is stored on the computer. It may look like an icon or a file. But it is not really working yet.\\n\\n### When the app wakes up\\nWhen you open the app, Linux wakes it up. Now the app becomes a live worker. It gets a number so Linux can know which worker it is. It gets some space to work. It gets turns to use the computer's brain.\\n\\nA browser, a drawing app, a terminal command, or a music app can all become live workers when opened.\\n\\n### Some workers are hidden\\nNot every worker stands in front of you. Some workers are quiet. For example, one helper may keep the internet ready. Another helper may wait for messages. You may not see them, but they can still be working.\\n\\n### Easy memory\\nApps are not running inside the icon. The icon is only like a button. When you press it, Linux starts the app as a live worker.\\n\\nSo remember: apps are stored when they are not opened, and they run as live workers when Linux starts them.", "questions": [{"question": "In the toy box example, what is an unopened app like?", "options": ["A toy car stored in a box", "A car already driving fast", "A flying bird", "A ringing phone"], "answer": "A toy car stored in a box"}, {"question": "What happens when you open an app in Linux?", "options": ["Linux starts it as a live worker", "The app turns into a pencil", "The computer becomes a book", "The screen becomes a chair"], "answer": "Linux starts it as a live worker"}, {"question": "Does an app really run inside its icon?", "options": ["No, the icon is only like a button", "Yes, the icon is the whole computer", "Yes, every icon has a tiny CPU inside", "No, icons can only play music"], "answer": "No, the icon is only like a button"}, {"question": "Why does Linux give a running app a number?", "options": ["To know which app is which", "To make it taste better", "To turn it blue", "To hide it forever"], "answer": "To know which app is which"}, {"question": "Which app can become a live worker?", "options": ["A music app", "A school shoe", "A lunch spoon", "A paper cup"], "answer": "A music app"}, {"question": "Can a hidden helper still be working?", "options": ["Yes, some helpers work quietly", "No, only loud helpers work", "Only if it is night", "Only if the mouse is sleeping"], "answer": "Yes, some helpers work quietly"}, {"question": "What is the computer's brain in the simple explanation?", "options": ["The part that carries out work", "The lunch box", "The table cloth", "The window glass"], "answer": "The part that carries out work"}, {"question": "Which statement is correct?", "options": ["An app can be stored first and run later", "An app is always running forever", "An app cannot be opened", "An app is only a chair"], "answer": "An app can be stored first and run later"}, {"question": "What may a quiet helper do?", "options": ["Keep the internet ready", "Eat the keyboard", "Paint the ceiling", "Fold the screen"], "answer": "Keep the internet ready"}, {"question": "What is the easiest way to remember where apps run?", "options": ["Linux starts apps as live workers", "Apps run inside stickers", "Apps run inside lunch plates", "Linux stores apps in clouds only"], "answer": "Linux starts apps as live workers"}]}}}	1
4	Userspace as Execution Area	userspace-as-execution-area	4	{"topic_name": "Userspace as Execution Area", "slug": "userspace-as-execution-area", "levels": {"standard": {"explanation": "## Userspace as Execution Area\\n\\n### Core idea\\nIn Linux, userspace is not just a place where applications are stored. It is an execution area: the environment where ordinary programs run as processes. When you start a command, open a graphical app, launch a server, or run a script, Linux creates one or more processes that execute in userspace.\\n\\nA userspace process has its own process ID, virtual memory, environment variables, file descriptors, permissions, current working directory, and parent-child relationship. It does not execute with full kernel authority. Instead, it runs in a controlled execution region and requests protected operations from the kernel when needed.\\n\\n### What execution area means\\nCalling userspace an execution area means:\\n\\n- Programs become active as processes there.\\n- Each process gets an isolated virtual address space.\\n- The kernel schedules userspace processes on the CPU.\\n- Userspace code cannot directly access kernel memory.\\n- File, device, network, and process operations require kernel-controlled interfaces.\\n\\nYou can inspect userspace processes with:\\n\\nbash\\nps -eo pid,ppid,user,stat,comm\\n\\n\\nYou can inspect one process through /proc:\\n\\nbash\\nls /proc/<PID>\\nreadlink /proc/<PID>/exe\\ncat /proc/<PID>/status\\n\\n\\nYou can observe active execution interactively with:\\n\\nbash\\ntop\\n\\n\\n### Boundary with the kernel\\nUserspace execution is active and powerful, but not unrestricted. For example, a userspace program can call a library function to read a file. That library function eventually asks the kernel to perform protected filesystem work. The program executes in userspace, while the kernel enforces permissions, manages hardware access, and returns results.\\n\\n### Key point\\nUserspace as an execution area means Linux separates where ordinary program code runs from where privileged operating-system work happens. This separation makes processes manageable, permissions enforceable, and application failures less dangerous to the whole system.", "questions": [{"question": "What does it mean to call userspace an execution area?", "options": ["It is where ordinary programs actively run as processes", "It is only a folder where application files are stored", "It is the physical area of RAM reserved for the monitor", "It is the firmware menu shown before Linux boots"], "answer": "It is where ordinary programs actively run as processes"}, {"question": "Which command can display PID, parent PID, user, state, and command name for processes?", "options": ["ps -eo pid,ppid,user,stat,comm", "mkfs.ext4 /dev/sda1", "fdisk -l /proc", "shutdown -r now"], "answer": "ps -eo pid,ppid,user,stat,comm"}, {"question": "Which property is normally associated with a userspace process?", "options": ["A process ID and virtual memory", "A permanent BIOS slot", "A physical keyboard wire", "A monitor-only register"], "answer": "A process ID and virtual memory"}, {"question": "What does /proc/<PID>/status help inspect?", "options": ["Status information about a running process", "The color profile of the desktop wallpaper", "The physical size of the CPU fan", "The bootloader theme configuration"], "answer": "Status information about a running process"}, {"question": "Why can a userspace process not directly access kernel memory?", "options": ["Linux enforces a protection boundary between userspace and kernel space", "Kernel memory is stored only inside the mouse", "Userspace programs are not allowed to use RAM", "The filesystem deletes kernel memory automatically"], "answer": "Linux enforces a protection boundary between userspace and kernel space"}, {"question": "Which action usually requires a userspace program to request kernel help?", "options": ["Reading a protected file from the filesystem", "Changing the spelling of a variable in source code", "Displaying a comment in a text editor", "Choosing a name for a local variable"], "answer": "Reading a protected file from the filesystem"}, {"question": "What role does the kernel play for userspace processes?", "options": ["It schedules them, manages resources, and enforces protected operations", "It converts every process into firmware", "It prevents all programs from running", "It stores all applications only as images"], "answer": "It schedules them, manages resources, and enforces protected operations"}, {"question": "Which command can show the executable file associated with a running process?", "options": ["readlink /proc//exe", "chmod /proc//exe", "mkfs /proc//exe", "reboot /proc//exe"], "answer": "readlink /proc//exe"}, {"question": "Which statement correctly describes userspace execution?", "options": ["The program runs in userspace while protected work is mediated by the kernel", "The program runs directly inside the disk controller", "The program becomes the kernel whenever it opens a file", "The program bypasses all permission checks automatically"], "answer": "The program runs in userspace while protected work is mediated by the kernel"}, {"question": "Why is userspace execution useful for system stability?", "options": ["A failure in an ordinary process is less likely to crash the whole system", "Every application receives full kernel authority", "All hardware becomes controlled by browser tabs", "The kernel stops managing memory"], "answer": "A failure in an ordinary process is less likely to crash the whole system"}]}, "layman": {"explanation": "## Userspace as Execution Area\\n\\n### Simple meaning\\nUserspace is the normal place where Linux programs do their work. It is not just where program files are kept. A program file is like a recipe written in a book. It is useful, but it is not cooking anything by itself. When Linux starts that program, it becomes a running activity. That running activity happens in userspace.\\n\\n### A workshop analogy\\nImagine a large workshop. Tools may be stored on shelves, but real work happens when a worker picks up a tool and starts using it. In Linux, the stored tool is like the program file. The worker doing the job is like the running process. Userspace is the workshop floor where these workers do normal jobs.\\n\\nMany kinds of Linux programs work there:\\n\\n- A browser opening websites\\n- A terminal running typed commands\\n- A text editor editing files\\n- A background service keeping the network ready\\n- A server waiting for requests\\n\\nSome of these are visible on the screen. Some are quiet. Both can still be running in userspace.\\n\\n### Why there are limits\\nThe workshop also has a locked control room. Only trusted people can touch the main power switch, safety controls, and heavy machines. In Linux, the kernel is like that control room. Normal programs work in userspace, but they must ask the kernel when they need protected work, such as opening a file, using the network, or starting another program.\\n\\nThis is useful because it keeps the computer organized. If one normal program makes a mistake, Linux tries to keep that mistake from damaging everything else. The program can fail, but the whole system should not always fail with it.\\n\\nA simple definition is: userspace is the normal working area where Linux programs run as live activities, while the kernel controls protected system work.", "questions": [{"question": "In the workshop analogy, what is userspace like?", "options": ["The workshop floor where normal work happens", "The locked control room only", "The storage box for broken tools", "The road outside the workshop"], "answer": "The workshop floor where normal work happens"}, {"question": "What is the difference between a program file and a running program?", "options": ["A program file is stored; a running program is actively doing work", "A program file is always more alive than a running program", "A running program must be printed on paper", "A program file controls the whole computer directly"], "answer": "A program file is stored; a running program is actively doing work"}, {"question": "Which example is a program doing work in userspace?", "options": ["A browser opening websites", "A chair beside a desk", "A disconnected power cable", "A paper label on a box"], "answer": "A browser opening websites"}, {"question": "Can a quiet background service still be running in userspace?", "options": ["Yes, it can run even without a visible window", "No, only programs with windows can run", "Only if the monitor is unplugged", "Only if it has no name"], "answer": "Yes, it can run even without a visible window"}, {"question": "What is the kernel like in the workshop analogy?", "options": ["The locked control room that handles protected work", "A worker's lunch plate", "A paint mark on the wall", "A small screw on the floor"], "answer": "The locked control room that handles protected work"}, {"question": "Why do normal programs ask the kernel for some jobs?", "options": ["Some jobs are protected and must be controlled safely", "Programs are not allowed to do any useful work", "The screen saves all files automatically", "The keyboard owns the hard disk"], "answer": "Some jobs are protected and must be controlled safely"}, {"question": "Which pair best describes the relationship?", "options": ["Userspace runs normal programs; the kernel controls protected work", "Userspace controls electricity; the kernel is a text file", "Userspace is a chair; the kernel is a window", "Userspace deletes apps; the kernel paints icons"], "answer": "Userspace runs normal programs; the kernel controls protected work"}, {"question": "Why is the separation between userspace and kernel useful?", "options": ["It helps prevent one program mistake from damaging the whole system", "It makes every program invisible", "It stops Linux from using memory", "It removes the need for applications"], "answer": "It helps prevent one program mistake from damaging the whole system"}, {"question": "Which statement is correct?", "options": ["Userspace is about where programs run, not just where files are stored", "Userspace means only a folder inside the home directory", "Userspace means only desktop wallpaper", "Userspace means the computer is turned off"], "answer": "Userspace is about where programs run, not just where files are stored"}, {"question": "Which simple definition is best?", "options": ["Userspace is the normal working area where Linux programs run", "Userspace is the place where cables are stored", "Userspace is only the name of a keyboard key", "Userspace is a special type of monitor"], "answer": "Userspace is the normal working area where Linux programs run"}]}, "eli10": {"explanation": "## Userspace as Execution Area\\n\\n### Imagine a classroom\\nThink of Linux like a classroom. Books can sit on a shelf, but they are not doing anything by themselves. Work starts when students open the books and begin reading or writing.\\n\\nApps are similar. Before you open an app, it is just stored on the computer. When Linux starts the app, the app becomes a worker doing a job. This working place is called userspace.\\n\\n### What happens there\\nIn userspace, normal apps do normal jobs:\\n\\n- A browser opens websites.\\n- A music app plays songs.\\n- A drawing app helps you draw.\\n- A terminal follows typed instructions.\\n- Some quiet helpers work in the background.\\n\\nYou may see some apps on the screen. Other helpers may be hidden, but they can still be working.\\n\\n### The teacher rule\\nIn the classroom, students should not open the locked office or touch the main electricity switch. They must ask the teacher. In Linux, the kernel is like the teacher. It protects important computer parts.\\n\\nSo apps work in userspace, but they ask the kernel when they need something important, like saving a file or using the internet.\\n\\n### Easy memory\\nUserspace is the normal work area for Linux apps. Apps do their jobs there, and the kernel protects the important parts of the computer.", "questions": [{"question": "In the classroom story, what is userspace like?", "options": ["The normal classroom area where work happens", "A locked cupboard only", "A school bus wheel", "A lunch box"], "answer": "The normal classroom area where work happens"}, {"question": "What is an app like before it is opened?", "options": ["A book sitting on a shelf", "A student already writing", "A bell already ringing", "A ball flying through the air"], "answer": "A book sitting on a shelf"}, {"question": "What happens when Linux starts an app?", "options": ["The app becomes a worker doing a job", "The app turns into a chair", "The computer becomes a notebook", "The screen turns into food"], "answer": "The app becomes a worker doing a job"}, {"question": "Which app does normal work in userspace?", "options": ["A drawing app", "A pencil sharpener", "A school shoe", "A water bottle"], "answer": "A drawing app"}, {"question": "Can some helpers work even if you do not see them?", "options": ["Yes, some helpers work quietly", "No, every helper must wave at you", "Only if it is raining", "Only if the screen is asleep"], "answer": "Yes, some helpers work quietly"}, {"question": "What is the kernel like in the classroom story?", "options": ["The teacher who protects important things", "A lunch spoon", "A football goal", "A school bag zipper"], "answer": "The teacher who protects important things"}, {"question": "Why do apps ask the kernel for important things?", "options": ["Because the kernel protects important computer parts", "Because apps cannot be opened", "Because the mouse owns all files", "Because the keyboard is the teacher"], "answer": "Because the kernel protects important computer parts"}, {"question": "Which thing might an app ask help for?", "options": ["Saving a file", "Eating lunch", "Wearing shoes", "Flying a kite"], "answer": "Saving a file"}, {"question": "Which sentence is correct?", "options": ["Apps do normal work in userspace", "Apps work only inside the desk", "Apps are always asleep", "Apps are the same as pencils"], "answer": "Apps do normal work in userspace"}, {"question": "What is the easiest memory for userspace?", "options": ["It is the normal work area for Linux apps", "It is a toy box under the bed", "It is only a picture on the wall", "It is a place where shoes run programs"], "answer": "It is the normal work area for Linux apps"}]}}}	1
5	Not Kernel Internals	not-kernel-internals	5	{"topic_name": "Not Kernel Internals", "slug": "not-kernel-internals", "levels": {"standard": {"explanation": "## Not Kernel Internals\\n\\n### Scope of this topic\\nIn a Linux userspace course, not kernel internals means the learner is not yet studying how the kernel itself is implemented. The focus is on understanding the boundary between ordinary programs and the kernel, not on reading scheduler code, memory-management algorithms, device-driver source, interrupt handlers, or architecture-specific kernel entry paths.\\n\\nUserspace learning answers questions like:\\n\\n- Where do normal programs run?\\n- How do commands, GUI apps, daemons, and servers behave as processes?\\n- When does a program need kernel help?\\n- How can we observe the boundary from the outside?\\n\\nKernel-internals learning asks deeper questions like:\\n\\n- How does the kernel scheduler choose the next task?\\n- How are page tables updated?\\n- How does a device driver handle interrupts?\\n- How is a system call dispatched inside the kernel?\\n\\n### What we can still inspect\\nEven without studying kernel internals, we can observe useful system facts from userspace:\\n\\nbash\\nuname -r\\n\\n\\nbash\\nps -eo pid,ppid,user,comm\\n\\n\\nbash\\ncat /proc/version\\n\\n\\nbash\\nstrace -e trace=openat,read,write ls\\n\\n\\nThese commands do not require the learner to understand kernel source code. They show the kernel version, running processes, exposed process/system information, and examples of userspace programs requesting kernel services.\\n\\n### Boundary-first learning\\nThe correct beginner-to-intermediate mental model is: applications run in userspace, and the kernel performs privileged operating-system work. A learner should first understand this boundary operationally before studying implementation details.\\n\\nFor example, when ls lists files, the course does not yet explain the full VFS implementation, inode lookup path, filesystem driver code, or block-device internals. Instead, it explains that ls runs in userspace and requests filesystem information through controlled kernel interfaces.\\n\\n### Key point\\nThis topic deliberately avoids deep kernel mechanisms. It builds the map needed before kernel internals: userspace programs run outside the kernel, request protected work through interfaces, and can be observed using normal Linux tools.", "questions": [{"question": "What does 'not kernel internals' mean in a userspace-focused Linux course?", "options": ["The course explains the userspace/kernel boundary without studying kernel implementation details", "The course ignores Linux completely", "The course teaches only BIOS configuration", "The course studies only physical CPU wiring"], "answer": "The course explains the userspace/kernel boundary without studying kernel implementation details"}, {"question": "Which topic belongs more to kernel internals than to a userspace introduction?", "options": ["How the scheduler selects the next task inside the kernel", "How to identify a running shell process", "How to classify Firefox as a GUI application", "How to distinguish a daemon from a visible app"], "answer": "How the scheduler selects the next task inside the kernel"}, {"question": "Which command can show the running kernel version without requiring kernel source-code knowledge?", "options": ["uname -r", "mkfs.ext4 /dev/sda1", "chmod 000 /", "shutdown -h now"], "answer": "uname -r"}, {"question": "Why is strace -e trace=openat,read,write ls useful in this topic?", "options": ["It shows examples of a userspace program requesting kernel services", "It prints the complete kernel scheduler source code", "It rebuilds the Linux kernel automatically", "It disables the syscall boundary"], "answer": "It shows examples of a userspace program requesting kernel services"}, {"question": "Which learning goal fits this topic best?", "options": ["Understand what lies outside the current scope when studying userspace first", "Memorize every line of the Linux memory manager", "Write a PCI device driver from scratch", "Implement a new CPU interrupt controller"], "answer": "Understand what lies outside the current scope when studying userspace first"}, {"question": "When teaching ls at this level, which explanation is most appropriate?", "options": ["ls runs in userspace and asks the kernel for filesystem information", "ls directly moves the disk head by itself", "ls replaces the filesystem driver", "ls runs only inside CPU firmware"], "answer": "ls runs in userspace and asks the kernel for filesystem information"}, {"question": "Which command can list ordinary running processes for boundary-focused observation?", "options": ["ps -eo pid,ppid,user,comm", "dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sda", "grub-install --force", "poweroff"], "answer": "ps -eo pid,ppid,user,comm"}, {"question": "Which item should be postponed until a dedicated kernel-internals course?", "options": ["Interrupt-handler implementation", "Userspace definition", "Program versus process distinction", "GUI app versus daemon classification"], "answer": "Interrupt-handler implementation"}, {"question": "What is the safest conceptual order for learners?", "options": ["Understand userspace and the boundary first, then study kernel internals later", "Start with page-table implementation before learning what a process is", "Ignore applications and study only device registers", "Avoid all commands until the kernel source is memorized"], "answer": "Understand userspace and the boundary first, then study kernel internals later"}, {"question": "Which statement is accurate for this topic?", "options": ["We can observe the kernel boundary from userspace without explaining every internal kernel mechanism", "Userspace courses must fully implement the kernel scheduler", "Normal Linux commands never interact with the kernel", "Kernel internals and desktop icons are the same subject"], "answer": "We can observe the kernel boundary from userspace without explaining every internal kernel mechanism"}]}, "layman": {"explanation": "## Not Kernel Internals\\n\\n### Simple meaning\\nThis topic means: we are learning where normal Linux programs live and how they reach the system core, but we are not opening the system core yet.\\n\\nThink of Linux as a large railway station. Passengers, ticket counters, signs, shops, and trains are visible parts of the station. A learner can first understand how people move, where tickets are checked, and how trains are announced. That does not mean the learner must immediately study the engine design, signal-circuit wiring, or how every control-room machine works inside.\\n\\nUserspace is like the public working area of the station. It includes normal Linux programs such as browsers, terminals, file managers, command-line tools, servers, and background services. The kernel is like the restricted control room. It manages important things, but this lesson does not explain the detailed machinery inside that room.\\n\\n### What we learn now\\nAt this stage, the learner should understand:\\n\\n- Normal programs run outside the kernel.\\n- Programs ask the kernel for protected work.\\n- Not every hidden thing is kernel internals.\\n- Background services can still be normal userspace software.\\n- The boundary matters before the internal machinery.\\n\\n### What we do not learn yet\\nWe are not yet studying how the kernel chooses CPU tasks, manages memory pages, talks to hardware devices, handles interrupts, or implements filesystems inside its code. Those are important topics, but they belong later.\\n\\n### Why this helps\\nIf a beginner starts directly with deep kernel machinery, Linux may feel confusing. It is better to first know the map: apps and tools run in userspace, the kernel handles protected work, and there is a controlled path between them.\\n\\nA simple definition is: not kernel internals means we understand the outside boundary and role of the kernel, without studying the detailed machinery inside the kernel yet.", "questions": [{"question": "In the railway station analogy, what is userspace like?", "options": ["The public working area where normal activity happens", "The hidden wiring inside the signal machine", "The engine's internal fuel system", "The steel inside the railway track"], "answer": "The public working area where normal activity happens"}, {"question": "What is the kernel like in the analogy?", "options": ["The restricted control room that manages important operations", "A passenger's bag", "A tea shop cup", "A station wall poster"], "answer": "The restricted control room that manages important operations"}, {"question": "What does this topic mainly teach?", "options": ["The boundary between normal programs and the kernel", "The full source code of Linux device drivers", "The electrical design of a CPU", "The physical structure of a hard disk platter"], "answer": "The boundary between normal programs and the kernel"}, {"question": "Which subject is too deep for this topic?", "options": ["How the kernel handles hardware interrupts internally", "That a browser is a normal program", "That a terminal command can run as a process", "That services may run in the background"], "answer": "How the kernel handles hardware interrupts internally"}, {"question": "Why should learners study the boundary before deep internals?", "options": ["It gives them a clear map before studying complex machinery", "It removes the need to learn Linux commands forever", "It proves that the kernel does not exist", "It makes all programs run without memory"], "answer": "It gives them a clear map before studying complex machinery"}, {"question": "Which statement is correct?", "options": ["A background service can still be normal userspace software", "Every hidden program is automatically kernel code", "Only visible windows belong to userspace", "A server must always be inside the CPU hardware"], "answer": "A background service can still be normal userspace software"}, {"question": "What should a normal program do when it needs protected work?", "options": ["Ask the kernel through a controlled path", "Open the hardware directly without rules", "Become a power cable", "Turn into a BIOS screen"], "answer": "Ask the kernel through a controlled path"}, {"question": "Which pair best matches the lesson?", "options": ["Userspace outside, kernel protected inside", "Keyboard outside, browser inside the battery", "Mouse outside, files inside the screen glass", "Wallpaper outside, terminal inside a table"], "answer": "Userspace outside, kernel protected inside"}, {"question": "What is postponed until later learning?", "options": ["Detailed kernel machinery", "Basic userspace meaning", "Program role classification", "The idea that apps ask for system help"], "answer": "Detailed kernel machinery"}, {"question": "Which simple definition is best?", "options": ["We learn the kernel's role and boundary, not its deep internal machinery yet", "We learn only how to paint desktop icons", "We learn that Linux has no protected system area", "We learn that apps never need the kernel"], "answer": "We learn the kernel's role and boundary, not its deep internal machinery yet"}]}, "eli10": {"explanation": "## Not Kernel Internals\\n\\n### A simple story\\nImagine a school. Students, teachers, classrooms, the library, and the playground are easy to see. You can learn how the school works by watching where students go, where teachers help, and where rules are followed.\\n\\nBut the school also has special rooms. There may be an office, a power room, and a record room. You do not need to open every machine in those rooms on the first day. First, you only need to know that those rooms exist and that they handle important jobs.\\n\\n### Linux idea\\nIn Linux, normal apps are like students doing work. They run in the normal area called userspace. The kernel is like the special office that protects important computer things.\\n\\nThis topic does not teach the tiny inside details of the kernel. It does not explain how the kernel talks to every device or how it chooses every computer task. That can come later.\\n\\n### What to remember\\nFor now, remember this:\\n\\n- Apps do normal work.\\n- The kernel protects important parts.\\n- Apps ask the kernel for special help.\\n- We are learning the map first, not opening every machine inside the office.\\n\\nSo, not kernel internals means we learn where the kernel is in the Linux story, but we do not study all its deep inside parts yet.", "questions": [{"question": "In the school story, what are normal apps like?", "options": ["Students doing normal work", "Clouds above the school", "Shoes under a bench", "A road outside the gate"], "answer": "Students doing normal work"}, {"question": "What is the kernel like in the school story?", "options": ["A special office that protects important things", "A lunch box", "A football", "A pencil cover"], "answer": "A special office that protects important things"}, {"question": "What does this topic avoid for now?", "options": ["The tiny inside details of the kernel", "The idea that apps do work", "The idea that Linux has programs", "The fact that computers can open apps"], "answer": "The tiny inside details of the kernel"}, {"question": "What should apps do when they need special help?", "options": ["Ask the kernel", "Break the computer", "Eat the keyboard", "Hide inside a shoe"], "answer": "Ask the kernel"}, {"question": "What are we learning first?", "options": ["The map of where apps and the kernel fit", "How to build every machine inside the computer", "How to open a real school office", "How to paint a monitor"], "answer": "The map of where apps and the kernel fit"}, {"question": "Which sentence is correct?", "options": ["We can know the kernel is important without studying all its inside parts yet", "The kernel is a toy car", "Apps never need help", "Linux has no important protected parts"], "answer": "We can know the kernel is important without studying all its inside parts yet"}, {"question": "Which thing belongs to later deeper learning?", "options": ["How the kernel talks to devices inside", "That apps do normal work", "That userspace is a normal app area", "That the kernel protects important parts"], "answer": "How the kernel talks to devices inside"}, {"question": "Why do we not open every machine in the special office first?", "options": ["Because it is better to understand the simple map first", "Because schools have no rooms", "Because apps are made of paper", "Because computers cannot run programs"], "answer": "Because it is better to understand the simple map first"}, {"question": "What does the kernel protect in the simple explanation?", "options": ["Important computer parts", "Only lunch plates", "Only playground balls", "Only school bags"], "answer": "Important computer parts"}, {"question": "What is the easiest meaning of not kernel internals?", "options": ["Learn the kernel's place, not all its inside details yet", "Forget that the kernel exists", "Learn only about chairs", "Say apps cannot run"], "answer": "Learn the kernel's place, not all its inside details yet"}]}}}	1
6	Above Syscall Boundary	above-syscall-boundary	6	{"topic_name": "Above Syscall Boundary", "slug": "above-syscall-boundary", "levels": {"standard": {"explanation": "## Above Syscall Boundary\\n\\n### Core idea\\nIn Linux, the phrase above the syscall boundary refers to the userspace side of the system: the area where normal programs execute before they request privileged work from the kernel. A syscall, or system call, is a controlled entry point from userspace into kernel space. Programs above this boundary include shells, command-line tools, graphical applications, servers, daemons, language runtimes, and libraries.\\n\\n### What runs above the boundary\\nAbove the syscall boundary, software runs as ordinary processes. Examples include:\\n\\n- bash, zsh, and other shells\\n- commands such as ls, grep, cat, and chmod\\n- graphical apps such as browsers and editors\\n- services such as sshd, cron, and nginx\\n- runtimes such as Python, Java, and Node.js\\n- libraries such as glibc that wrap lower-level system calls\\n\\nA command like ls does not directly inspect disk blocks by itself. It runs above the boundary, asks for filesystem information through library functions, and those functions eventually invoke system calls that enter the kernel.\\n\\n### Observing the boundary\\nYou can observe userspace programs and their syscall activity with commands such as:\\n\\nbash\\nps -eo pid,user,comm\\n\\n\\nbash\\nstrace -e trace=openat,read,write ls\\n\\n\\nbash\\nstrace -c cat /etc/hostname\\n\\n\\nstrace shows system calls made by a userspace program. This is useful because it exposes the crossing point without requiring the learner to study kernel source code.\\n\\n### Why this matters\\nThe syscall boundary protects the system. Code above the boundary cannot freely access kernel memory, disk devices, network hardware, or process-management internals. It must request privileged operations through defined interfaces. The kernel then checks permissions, manages resources, performs the protected operation, and returns a result.\\n\\nA useful summary is: above the syscall boundary means normal Linux software is still running in userspace; it can do application work directly, but it must cross a controlled boundary when it needs protected kernel services.", "questions": [{"question": "What does 'above the syscall boundary' mainly refer to in Linux?", "options": ["The userspace side where normal programs run before requesting kernel services", "The physical area above the CPU chip", "The BIOS configuration screen before Linux boots", "The disk partition where swap files are stored"], "answer": "The userspace side where normal programs run before requesting kernel services"}, {"question": "What is a syscall in Linux?", "options": ["A controlled entry point from userspace into kernel space", "A command that changes the desktop wallpaper", "A file extension used only by shell scripts", "A hardware cable connected to the network card"], "answer": "A controlled entry point from userspace into kernel space"}, {"question": "Which command can show syscall activity for the ls program?", "options": ["strace -e trace=openat,read,write ls", "chmod -R 000 /", "mkfs.ext4 /dev/sda1", "shutdown now"], "answer": "strace -e trace=openat,read,write ls"}, {"question": "Which software usually runs above the syscall boundary?", "options": ["bash", "Kernel page-fault handler", "Device interrupt handler inside the kernel", "Kernel scheduler core"], "answer": "bash"}, {"question": "Why does ls need to cross the syscall boundary?", "options": ["It needs kernel-mediated filesystem information", "It directly controls the disk motor without Linux", "It runs only inside BIOS memory", "It must replace the shell before listing files"], "answer": "It needs kernel-mediated filesystem information"}, {"question": "Which command can list ordinary processes running above the syscall boundary?", "options": ["ps -eo pid,user,comm", "fdisk --delete-all", "grub-install /dev/sda", "poweroff --force"], "answer": "ps -eo pid,user,comm"}, {"question": "What does the kernel usually do after a userspace program makes a syscall?", "options": ["Checks permissions, performs protected work, and returns a result", "Deletes the program automatically", "Turns the process into firmware", "Removes the CPU from scheduling"], "answer": "Checks permissions, performs protected work, and returns a result"}, {"question": "Which statement about code above the syscall boundary is accurate?", "options": ["It can perform normal application work but cannot freely access protected kernel resources", "It has unrestricted access to all kernel memory", "It is always part of a device driver", "It runs only when Linux is powered off"], "answer": "It can perform normal application work but cannot freely access protected kernel resources"}, {"question": "Why are libraries such as glibc important in this context?", "options": ["They often provide convenient wrappers around lower-level system calls", "They replace the Linux kernel permanently", "They directly connect applications to raw CPU pins", "They prevent applications from using files"], "answer": "They often provide convenient wrappers around lower-level system calls"}, {"question": "Which result does strace -c cat /etc/hostname provide?", "options": ["A summary of system calls made while running the command", "A full rebuild of the Linux kernel", "A graphical map of disk partitions", "A password reset for the current user"], "answer": "A summary of system calls made while running the command"}]}, "layman": {"explanation": "## Above Syscall Boundary\\n\\n### Simple meaning\\nThink of Linux as a building with two main areas. The upper working area is where normal people do ordinary jobs. The lower restricted control room is where important building systems are handled. The line between these two areas is like the syscall boundary.\\n\\nBeing above the syscall boundary means a program is still in the normal working area. A browser, terminal, text editor, file manager, music app, server, or background service can work there. These programs can do many useful things, but they cannot directly touch every protected part of the computer.\\n\\n### Asking through the gate\\nSuppose a worker wants to open a locked storage room. The worker cannot just break the door. They must ask the security desk. In Linux, a normal program does something similar. If it wants to read a file, save data, use the network, or start another program, it asks the kernel through a controlled gate. That gate is the syscall boundary.\\n\\nThe word syscall means a system request. It is how a normal program asks the Linux kernel to perform protected work. The program stays above the boundary while doing normal work, but crosses the boundary briefly when it needs help from the kernel.\\n\\n### Why this is useful\\nThis design keeps the system safer. If every program could directly control files, memory, devices, and the network, one mistake could damage the whole machine. The boundary makes Linux more organized because the kernel can check permissions and decide whether the requested action is allowed.\\n\\n### Important idea\\nAbove the syscall boundary does not mean weak or useless. Powerful programs such as browsers, servers, and shells run there. It only means they are normal programs that must ask the kernel before doing protected system work.\\n\\nA simple definition is: above the syscall boundary means the program is running in the normal Linux program area and must ask the kernel when it needs protected help.", "questions": [{"question": "In the building analogy, what is above the syscall boundary like?", "options": ["The normal working area where people do ordinary jobs", "The locked control room only", "The foundation stones under the building", "The roof tiles outside the computer"], "answer": "The normal working area where people do ordinary jobs"}, {"question": "What is the syscall boundary like in simple words?", "options": ["A controlled gate between normal programs and protected kernel work", "A computer screen border", "A file name ending", "A keyboard shortcut for opening apps"], "answer": "A controlled gate between normal programs and protected kernel work"}, {"question": "Which program can run above the syscall boundary?", "options": ["A text editor", "A wooden table", "A power plug", "A monitor stand"], "answer": "A text editor"}, {"question": "What should a normal program do when it needs to read a protected file?", "options": ["Ask the kernel through the controlled gate", "Break the system rules and take the file directly", "Turn into a keyboard", "Erase the whole operating system"], "answer": "Ask the kernel through the controlled gate"}, {"question": "Why does Linux use a syscall boundary?", "options": ["To keep protected system work controlled and safer", "To stop all applications from running", "To make every app invisible", "To remove the need for files"], "answer": "To keep protected system work controlled and safer"}, {"question": "What does syscall mean in simple language?", "options": ["A system request made by a normal program", "A type of computer chair", "A screen brightness setting", "A name for a broken application"], "answer": "A system request made by a normal program"}, {"question": "Which action may require crossing the syscall boundary?", "options": ["Saving data to a file", "Thinking of a variable name before coding", "Reading a printed book beside the computer", "Looking at the color of the monitor frame"], "answer": "Saving data to a file"}, {"question": "Does being above the syscall boundary mean a program is useless?", "options": ["No, powerful programs can still run there", "Yes, only useless programs run there", "Yes, it means the program cannot work", "No, it means the program is hardware"], "answer": "No, powerful programs can still run there"}, {"question": "What can the kernel do when a program asks for protected work?", "options": ["Check whether the request is allowed", "Always delete the program", "Turn the screen into a disk", "Remove the user's keyboard"], "answer": "Check whether the request is allowed"}, {"question": "Which simple definition is best?", "options": ["Above the syscall boundary means normal programs run there and ask the kernel for protected help", "Above the syscall boundary means programs live inside the monitor", "Above the syscall boundary means Linux has no kernel", "Above the syscall boundary means all programs become device drivers"], "answer": "Above the syscall boundary means normal programs run there and ask the kernel for protected help"}]}, "eli10": {"explanation": "## Above Syscall Boundary\\n\\n### A simple story\\nImagine a school. Students can work in classrooms. They can read, write, draw, and talk to the teacher. But some places are special, like the office, the record room, and the electricity room. Students cannot just walk in and touch everything.\\n\\nIn Linux, normal apps are like students. They work in the normal area. This normal area is above the syscall boundary.\\n\\n### Asking for help\\nIf a student needs something from the locked office, they must ask the teacher. In Linux, apps must ask the kernel when they need important computer help.\\n\\nFor example, an app may need to:\\n\\n- save a file\\n- read a file\\n- use the internet\\n- start another app\\n\\nThe app cannot just grab everything by itself. It asks through a special gate. That gate is called the syscall boundary.\\n\\n### Why the gate matters\\nThe gate keeps the computer safer. If every app could touch every important part, one bad app could make a big mess.\\n\\nSo remember: above the syscall boundary means normal apps are doing normal work, but they must ask the kernel before touching important computer things.", "questions": [{"question": "In the school story, what are normal apps like?", "options": ["Students working in classrooms", "Clouds above the school", "Shoes near the door", "A football goal"], "answer": "Students working in classrooms"}, {"question": "What is above the syscall boundary like?", "options": ["The normal area where apps work", "A lunch box", "A broken pencil", "A playground slide"], "answer": "The normal area where apps work"}, {"question": "What is the kernel like in the simple story?", "options": ["The teacher who protects important things", "A school bag", "A water bottle", "A wall clock"], "answer": "The teacher who protects important things"}, {"question": "What should an app do before touching important computer things?", "options": ["Ask the kernel", "Break the computer", "Hide under the keyboard", "Turn into paper"], "answer": "Ask the kernel"}, {"question": "Which task may need help from the kernel?", "options": ["Saving a file", "Eating a snack", "Tying a shoe", "Opening a lunch box"], "answer": "Saving a file"}, {"question": "Why is there a special gate?", "options": ["To keep important computer parts safer", "To make all apps disappear", "To stop the screen from showing colors", "To turn files into toys"], "answer": "To keep important computer parts safer"}, {"question": "Can apps do normal work above the syscall boundary?", "options": ["Yes, apps can do normal work there", "No, apps cannot work there", "Only if the computer is asleep", "Only if the app is a shoe"], "answer": "Yes, apps can do normal work there"}, {"question": "What might happen if every app could touch everything?", "options": ["One bad app could make a big mess", "The computer would become a sandwich", "All apps would turn into pencils", "The keyboard would become a book"], "answer": "One bad app could make a big mess"}, {"question": "Which sentence is correct?", "options": ["Apps ask the kernel for important help", "Apps are always the kernel", "The kernel is a lunch spoon", "Files are kept only inside the mouse"], "answer": "Apps ask the kernel for important help"}, {"question": "What is the easiest meaning of above the syscall boundary?", "options": ["Normal apps work there and ask before touching important parts", "Apps sleep inside a toy box", "Linux has no special protected parts", "The screen controls all files alone"], "answer": "Normal apps work there and ask before touching important parts"}]}}}	1
7	Visible and Invisible Software	visible-and-invisible-software	7	{"topic_name": "Visible and Invisible Software", "slug": "visible-and-invisible-software", "levels": {"standard": {"explanation": "## Visible and Invisible Software in Linux\\n\\n### Core idea\\nLinux userspace contains both visible software and invisible software. Visible software is easy to notice because it has a window, terminal output, menu entry, or active user interface. Examples include Firefox, LibreOffice, a terminal emulator, a text editor, or a file manager. Invisible software usually runs without a direct window. It may work in the background as a daemon, service, helper process, server, runtime component, or session support process.\\n\\n### Visibility is not the same as existence\\nA common mistake is to think that only software shown on the screen is running. In Linux, many important userspace programs run quietly. For example, sshd can wait for remote login requests, cron can schedule jobs, NetworkManager can manage network connections, and nginx can respond to web requests. These programs may not open a visible window, but they are still real running processes.\\n\\nYou can inspect visible and invisible processes using commands such as:\\n\\nbash\\nps -eo pid,ppid,user,stat,comm\\n\\n\\nbash\\ntop\\n\\n\\nbash\\nsystemctl status sshd\\n\\n\\nbash\\npgrep -a nginx\\n\\n\\n### Foreground and background behavior\\nA foreground program usually receives direct user attention. A background program may continue running while the user does something else. However, both can still be userspace processes. A terminal command like top is visible in the terminal, while a service like sshd may run without visible output.\\n\\n### Why invisible software matters\\nInvisible software makes Linux usable as a system. Networking, printing, scheduled tasks, graphical sessions, login handling, audio, web serving, and remote access often depend on background processes. These processes are not less important simply because they are not visible.\\n\\n### Key point\\nVisible software and invisible software are both part of the Linux userspace world. Visibility tells us how the user experiences the program, not whether the program is real, active, or important.", "questions": [{"question": "What is the best definition of invisible software in Linux userspace?", "options": ["Software that runs without a direct visible window or active user interface", "Software that cannot be executed by the kernel", "Software stored only inside the monitor hardware", "Software that has been permanently deleted"], "answer": "Software that runs without a direct visible window or active user interface"}, {"question": "Which command can show both visible and background processes with PID and command name?", "options": ["ps -eo pid,ppid,user,stat,comm", "mkfs.ext4 /dev/sda1", "fdisk --wipe /dev/sda", "shutdown -h now"], "answer": "ps -eo pid,ppid,user,stat,comm"}, {"question": "Which example is usually invisible background userspace software?", "options": ["sshd waiting for remote login connections", "A visible LibreOffice document window", "A user moving the mouse pointer", "The desktop wallpaper image"], "answer": "sshd waiting for remote login connections"}, {"question": "Which command is suitable for checking the status of an sshd service on a systemd-based Linux system?", "options": ["systemctl status sshd", "chmod status sshd", "mount status sshd", "mkfs status sshd"], "answer": "systemctl status sshd"}, {"question": "Which statement about visibility and process existence is correct?", "options": ["A process can be running even if it has no visible window", "Only windows shown on the screen are real processes", "Invisible software always belongs to kernel space", "Background services cannot use CPU time"], "answer": "A process can be running even if it has no visible window"}, {"question": "Which command can search for running nginx processes with command-line details?", "options": ["pgrep -a nginx", "rm -rf nginx", "umount nginx", "fdisk nginx"], "answer": "pgrep -a nginx"}, {"question": "Which item is an example of visible userspace software?", "options": ["A terminal emulator window", "A kernel interrupt handler", "A CPU cache line", "A disk partition table"], "answer": "A terminal emulator window"}, {"question": "Why is invisible software important in Linux?", "options": ["It supports services such as networking, scheduling, remote access, and web serving", "It prevents all users from opening applications", "It exists only when the computer is powered off", "It replaces all visible programs with firmware"], "answer": "It supports services such as networking, scheduling, remote access, and web serving"}, {"question": "Which pair correctly contrasts foreground and background software?", "options": ["Foreground receives direct attention; background continues without constant visible interaction", "Foreground is always kernel code; background is always hardware", "Foreground means deleted; background means installed", "Foreground uses no memory; background uses no CPU"], "answer": "Foreground receives direct attention; background continues without constant visible interaction"}, {"question": "What does software visibility mainly describe?", "options": ["How the user experiences or notices the program", "Whether the software is physically inside the keyboard", "Whether the program is allowed to exist", "Whether the CPU has stopped running"], "answer": "How the user experiences or notices the program"}]}, "layman": {"explanation": "## Visible and Invisible Software\\n\\n### Simple meaning\\nIn Linux, some software is visible, and some software is invisible. Visible software is easy to notice because you can see it on the screen. A browser window, file manager, text editor, calculator app, and terminal window are visible examples.\\n\\nInvisible software is different. It may be working quietly in the background without showing a window. This does not mean it is fake or unimportant. It only means you do not directly see it while it works.\\n\\n### A hotel analogy\\nThink of Linux like a hotel. Guests can see the receptionist, restaurant, rooms, and front desk. These are like visible apps. But many workers are behind the scenes. Someone manages electricity, someone handles laundry, someone watches security cameras, and someone keeps the internet working. Guests may not see them, but the hotel depends on them.\\n\\nLinux is similar. You may see the browser, but you may not see the network service helping the computer stay connected. You may see a terminal, but you may not see the background service waiting for remote login. You may open a website, but a server process can run quietly without a desktop window.\\n\\n### Why this matters\\nBeginners often think, \\u201cIf I cannot see it, it is not running.\\u201d In Linux, that idea is wrong. A program can run without a window. It can wait for requests, manage time-based jobs, handle network connections, or support the desktop session.\\n\\n### Visible does not mean more real\\nA visible app and an invisible service can both be real running programs. The difference is only how they appear to the user. One asks for attention on the screen. The other quietly performs a job.\\n\\nA simple definition is: visible software is software you can directly see, while invisible software runs quietly in the background but still does real work.", "questions": [{"question": "What is visible software in Linux?", "options": ["Software the user can directly see or interact with on the screen", "Software hidden inside the computer table", "Software that cannot run", "Software stored only in the power cable"], "answer": "Software the user can directly see or interact with on the screen"}, {"question": "What is invisible software in simple terms?", "options": ["Software that works quietly without showing a window", "Software that has been erased forever", "Software that exists only in a printed book", "Software that runs inside the monitor glass"], "answer": "Software that works quietly without showing a window"}, {"question": "In the hotel analogy, what are visible apps like?", "options": ["The front desk and places guests can see", "Underground water pipes only", "A locked box under the floor", "The bricks inside the wall"], "answer": "The front desk and places guests can see"}, {"question": "In the hotel analogy, what are invisible services like?", "options": ["Behind-the-scenes workers who keep the hotel running", "Guests eating in the restaurant", "A signboard outside the hotel", "A chair in the lobby"], "answer": "Behind-the-scenes workers who keep the hotel running"}, {"question": "Which example is visible software?", "options": ["A browser window", "A hidden network service", "A scheduled background job", "A remote login service waiting quietly"], "answer": "A browser window"}, {"question": "Which example is invisible software?", "options": ["A service quietly managing network connection", "A calculator window shown on screen", "A text editor window", "A file manager window"], "answer": "A service quietly managing network connection"}, {"question": "Which beginner idea is wrong?", "options": ["If I cannot see it, it is not running", "A browser can show a window", "Some programs run in the background", "Linux can have services"], "answer": "If I cannot see it, it is not running"}, {"question": "Why can invisible software be important?", "options": ["It can manage useful jobs such as networking, scheduled work, or remote access", "It always stops the computer from working", "It means Linux has no visible apps", "It is only a picture hidden behind wallpaper"], "answer": "It can manage useful jobs such as networking, scheduled work, or remote access"}, {"question": "What is the main difference between visible and invisible software?", "options": ["How directly the user can notice or interact with it", "Whether it is real or fake", "Whether it is allowed to use electricity", "Whether it is stored in a keyboard"], "answer": "How directly the user can notice or interact with it"}, {"question": "Which simple definition is best?", "options": ["Visible software is seen directly; invisible software runs quietly but still works", "Visible software is real; invisible software is imaginary", "Visible software is hardware; invisible software is furniture", "Visible software is deleted; invisible software is installed"], "answer": "Visible software is seen directly; invisible software runs quietly but still works"}]}, "eli10": {"explanation": "## Visible and Invisible Software\\n\\n### A simple story\\nImagine a school. Some people are easy to see. You see the teacher in class. You see students reading. You see the person at the front office. These are like visible apps in Linux.\\n\\nBut some people help quietly. Someone rings the bell. Someone keeps the lights working. Someone cleans the rooms. Someone checks the gate. You may not see them all the time, but the school needs them.\\n\\n### Linux idea\\nLinux also has visible and invisible helpers.\\n\\nVisible helpers are apps you can see, such as:\\n\\n- a browser\\n- a drawing app\\n- a music app\\n- a terminal window\\n\\nInvisible helpers work quietly. They may keep the internet ready, wait for messages, or do jobs at the right time. You may not see a window, but they can still be working.\\n\\n### Easy memory\\nDo not think, \\u201cI cannot see it, so it is not working.\\u201d Some important helpers are quiet.\\n\\nA simple way to remember it is: visible software works where you can see it, and invisible software works quietly in the background.", "questions": [{"question": "In the school story, what are visible apps like?", "options": ["People you can easily see doing work", "Clouds over the school", "Shoes under a bench", "A closed lunch box"], "answer": "People you can easily see doing work"}, {"question": "What are invisible helpers like?", "options": ["People who help quietly in the background", "A football on the field", "A pencil on the desk", "A window curtain"], "answer": "People who help quietly in the background"}, {"question": "Which one is visible software?", "options": ["A browser window", "A hidden helper keeping internet ready", "A quiet job waiting for time", "A service waiting for messages"], "answer": "A browser window"}, {"question": "Which one is invisible software?", "options": ["A quiet helper keeping the internet ready", "A drawing app window", "A music app window", "A visible terminal window"], "answer": "A quiet helper keeping the internet ready"}, {"question": "If you cannot see a helper, can it still be working?", "options": ["Yes, it can still be working quietly", "No, invisible things never work", "Only if the screen is blue", "Only if the keyboard is asleep"], "answer": "Yes, it can still be working quietly"}, {"question": "What might an invisible helper do?", "options": ["Wait for messages", "Eat a lunch box", "Paint the school wall", "Tie a shoe"], "answer": "Wait for messages"}, {"question": "Which thought is wrong?", "options": ["If I cannot see it, it cannot be running", "Some helpers are visible", "Some helpers are quiet", "A browser can be seen"], "answer": "If I cannot see it, it cannot be running"}, {"question": "Why are quiet helpers useful?", "options": ["They do important jobs without asking for attention", "They stop every app from opening", "They turn the computer into paper", "They make files disappear forever"], "answer": "They do important jobs without asking for attention"}, {"question": "Which sentence is correct?", "options": ["Some Linux software is seen, and some works quietly", "Only visible software is real", "Invisible software is always broken", "Linux has no quiet helpers"], "answer": "Some Linux software is seen, and some works quietly"}, {"question": "What is the easiest meaning of visible and invisible software?", "options": ["Some apps are seen, and some helpers work quietly", "All apps must shout loudly", "Hidden helpers are toys", "Visible apps are cables"], "answer": "Some apps are seen, and some helpers work quietly"}]}}}	1
8	Why Userspace Matters	why-userspace-matters	8	{"topic_name": "Why Userspace Matters", "slug": "why-userspace-matters", "levels": {"standard": {"explanation": "## Why Userspace Matters\\n\\n### Core idea\\nUserspace matters because it is the main execution environment where most Linux activity begins. When a user opens a browser, runs ls, starts Python, edits a file, launches nginx, or logs in through sshd, the active software normally runs as userspace processes. The kernel provides privileged operating-system services, but userspace is where applications, tools, services, runtimes, and user-facing workflows actually live.\\n\\n### Why this separation is important\\nLinux separates userspace from kernel space for several reasons:\\n\\n- Safety: a broken application should not automatically damage the entire operating system.\\n- Security: ordinary programs should not directly access protected memory, devices, or files.\\n- Stability: the kernel can enforce boundaries between processes.\\n- Manageability: programs can be started, stopped, inspected, signaled, and monitored as processes.\\n- Abstraction: apps can use libraries and system interfaces instead of directly managing hardware.\\n\\nYou can observe userspace as a process world with:\\n\\nbash\\nps -eo pid,ppid,user,stat,comm\\n\\n\\nYou can monitor active processes with:\\n\\nbash\\ntop\\n\\n\\nYou can inspect a specific running program with:\\n\\nbash\\npgrep -a sshd\\nreadlink /proc/<PID>/exe\\ncat /proc/<PID>/status\\n\\n\\n### Practical importance\\nUserspace is the learner's main entry point into Linux. Commands, shells, graphical apps, daemons, servers, package tools, scripting languages, and desktop components all make sense once the learner sees them as userspace software. This helps prevent the beginner mistake of treating Linux as only terminal commands or only hidden kernel behavior.\\n\\nUserspace also matters because it is where the syscall boundary becomes meaningful. A userspace program can do normal computation itself, but when it needs protected work such as reading a file, opening a socket, creating a process, or using a device, it must request kernel help through controlled interfaces.\\n\\n### Key point\\nUnderstanding userspace gives the learner a durable map of Linux: ordinary programs run in userspace, the kernel controls privileged work, and the boundary between them protects the whole system.", "questions": [{"question": "Why is userspace important in Linux?", "options": ["It is where most ordinary applications, tools, services, and workflows run", "It is the physical area where the CPU is installed", "It is the BIOS menu used before booting", "It is the disk area reserved only for deleted files"], "answer": "It is where most ordinary applications, tools, services, and workflows run"}, {"question": "Which command can show userspace processes with PID, parent PID, user, state, and command name?", "options": ["ps -eo pid,ppid,user,stat,comm", "mkfs.ext4 /dev/sda1", "shutdown -h now", "fdisk --delete /dev/sda"], "answer": "ps -eo pid,ppid,user,stat,comm"}, {"question": "Which benefit comes from separating userspace from kernel space?", "options": ["A faulty ordinary application is less likely to damage the whole operating system", "Every application gets unrestricted kernel memory access", "The system no longer needs permissions", "All hardware becomes controlled directly by browsers"], "answer": "A faulty ordinary application is less likely to damage the whole operating system"}, {"question": "Which group best represents userspace software?", "options": ["bash, Firefox, Python, nginx, sshd", "Kernel scheduler, page-fault handler, interrupt handler, block driver", "CPU pins, RAM chips, disk platters, keyboard switches", "BIOS setup, firmware ROM, boot splash, hardware clock"], "answer": "bash, Firefox, Python, nginx, sshd"}, {"question": "Why does userspace help with manageability?", "options": ["Programs can be inspected, monitored, started, stopped, and signaled as processes", "Programs cannot be observed after they start", "All processes disappear from the system table", "The kernel stops assigning process IDs"], "answer": "Programs can be inspected, monitored, started, stopped, and signaled as processes"}, {"question": "Which command can monitor active processes interactively?", "options": ["top", "pwd", "echo test", "touch file.txt"], "answer": "top"}, {"question": "What does userspace help beginners avoid?", "options": ["The mistaken idea that Linux is only terminal commands or only kernel internals", "The need to learn what a process is", "The ability to run applications", "The existence of shells and graphical programs"], "answer": "The mistaken idea that Linux is only terminal commands or only kernel internals"}, {"question": "Why does a userspace program use controlled interfaces for protected operations?", "options": ["The kernel must check permissions and safely manage protected resources", "Userspace programs are unable to perform any computation", "Files are stored only inside terminal windows", "The shell directly owns all hardware devices"], "answer": "The kernel must check permissions and safely manage protected resources"}, {"question": "Which command can show the executable linked to a running process?", "options": ["readlink /proc//exe", "chmod /proc//exe", "mkfs /proc//exe", "reboot /proc//exe"], "answer": "readlink /proc//exe"}, {"question": "Which statement best summarizes why userspace matters?", "options": ["It provides the ordinary program world while the kernel protects privileged system work", "It replaces the kernel entirely", "It is only a graphical desktop theme", "It allows every app to bypass system rules"], "answer": "It provides the ordinary program world while the kernel protects privileged system work"}]}, "layman": {"explanation": "## Why Userspace Matters\\n\\n### Simple meaning\\nUserspace matters because it is where normal Linux programs do their everyday work. Think of Linux as a large city. The city has shops, offices, schools, buses, workers, delivery people, and service centers. These are like normal Linux programs: browsers, terminals, file managers, editors, background services, and servers.\\n\\nThe city also has a control authority that manages important things such as electricity, roads, water, and safety rules. In Linux, that control authority is like the kernel. The kernel protects important computer resources, while userspace is where normal programs live and work.\\n\\n### Why this is useful\\nIf every shop or worker could directly control the power station, traffic lights, bank vaults, and water supply, the city would be unsafe. One careless person could create a big problem. Linux avoids this by keeping normal programs in userspace and making them ask the kernel for protected work.\\n\\nThis gives Linux several advantages:\\n\\n- One app crashing should not automatically crash the whole system.\\n- Programs can be identified and managed separately.\\n- Files, memory, devices, and network access can be protected.\\n- Background services can quietly do useful work.\\n- Visible apps and invisible helpers can be understood as part of one software world.\\n\\n### Why learners need this idea\\nWithout userspace, Linux feels like a random collection of names: browser, shell, daemon, server, command, library, and kernel. Userspace connects these ideas. A browser window, a terminal command, and a background service may look different, but they can all be normal programs running in the same broad userspace area.\\n\\n### Simple definition\\nUserspace matters because it gives Linux a safe and organized place for normal programs to run, while the kernel protects important system work.", "questions": [{"question": "In the city analogy, what is userspace like?", "options": ["The normal city area where people and services do everyday work", "The locked power station only", "The stones under the road", "The clouds above the city"], "answer": "The normal city area where people and services do everyday work"}, {"question": "In the same analogy, what is the kernel like?", "options": ["The control authority that protects important city systems", "A shop sign", "A passenger seat", "A street lamp color"], "answer": "The control authority that protects important city systems"}, {"question": "Why should normal programs not directly control everything?", "options": ["One mistake could create a serious system problem", "Programs are not allowed to do useful work", "Linux has no files to protect", "Screens control all computer resources"], "answer": "One mistake could create a serious system problem"}, {"question": "Which item belongs to the normal userspace world?", "options": ["A file manager", "A wall clock", "A power plug", "A wooden chair"], "answer": "A file manager"}, {"question": "What is one reason userspace improves organization?", "options": ["Programs can be treated as separate running activities", "All programs become one single invisible object", "The computer stops using memory", "Apps no longer need names"], "answer": "Programs can be treated as separate running activities"}, {"question": "What should a userspace app do when it needs protected work?", "options": ["Ask the kernel through the proper path", "Directly take control of all hardware", "Erase the operating system", "Turn into a keyboard"], "answer": "Ask the kernel through the proper path"}, {"question": "Which statement is correct?", "options": ["Visible apps and quiet background services can both belong to userspace", "Only windows on the screen are real programs", "Background services are always physical hardware", "Servers cannot be normal Linux programs"], "answer": "Visible apps and quiet background services can both belong to userspace"}, {"question": "Why is userspace useful for beginners learning Linux?", "options": ["It connects apps, commands, services, servers, and libraries into one clear picture", "It hides all programs from the learner", "It removes the need to understand applications", "It proves the kernel is unnecessary"], "answer": "It connects apps, commands, services, servers, and libraries into one clear picture"}, {"question": "What can happen if one normal app crashes?", "options": ["Linux tries to keep the rest of the system working", "The computer must physically break", "All other apps must become files", "The keyboard must stop existing"], "answer": "Linux tries to keep the rest of the system working"}, {"question": "Which simple definition is best?", "options": ["Userspace is the safe normal area where Linux programs run", "Userspace is only the mouse pointer", "Userspace is the inside of a battery", "Userspace is a deleted folder"], "answer": "Userspace is the safe normal area where Linux programs run"}]}, "eli10": {"explanation": "## Why Userspace Matters\\n\\n### A simple story\\nImagine a school. Students study in classrooms. Teachers teach lessons. The librarian keeps books. The office keeps records. The watchman checks the gate. Many people do different jobs.\\n\\nLinux also has many helpers. A browser opens websites. A music app plays songs. A terminal follows typed instructions. Some quiet helpers keep the internet ready or wait for messages. These normal helpers work in userspace.\\n\\n### Why not let everyone touch everything?\\nIn a school, students should not open the electricity room or change all school records by themselves. They must ask the right person. This keeps the school safe.\\n\\nLinux works in a similar way. Normal apps do their jobs in userspace. When they need something important, like saving a file or using the internet, they ask the kernel. The kernel is like the trusted helper that protects important computer parts.\\n\\n### Why this helps\\nUserspace matters because it keeps normal apps in their proper working area. If one app gets stuck, the whole computer should not always stop. If one app asks for something important, the kernel can check whether it is allowed.\\n\\nEasy memory: userspace is where normal Linux apps work safely, and the kernel protects the important parts.", "questions": [{"question": "In the school story, what are Linux apps like?", "options": ["Helpers doing different jobs", "Clouds above the school", "Shoes near the door", "A lunch plate"], "answer": "Helpers doing different jobs"}, {"question": "Where do normal Linux helpers work?", "options": ["In userspace", "Inside a lunch box", "On a playground slide", "Inside a pencil"], "answer": "In userspace"}, {"question": "What is the kernel like in the story?", "options": ["A trusted helper that protects important things", "A school shoe", "A football", "A window curtain"], "answer": "A trusted helper that protects important things"}, {"question": "Why should students not touch the electricity room by themselves?", "options": ["It could be unsafe", "It is made of candy", "It is only for playing games", "It is the same as a notebook"], "answer": "It could be unsafe"}, {"question": "What should an app do when it needs important help?", "options": ["Ask the kernel", "Break the computer", "Eat the keyboard", "Hide inside a shoe"], "answer": "Ask the kernel"}, {"question": "Which helper can work in userspace?", "options": ["A music app", "A water bottle", "A lunch spoon", "A school bag"], "answer": "A music app"}, {"question": "What should ideally happen if one app gets stuck?", "options": ["The whole computer should not always stop", "Every app must turn into paper", "The monitor must become a chair", "All files must become toys"], "answer": "The whole computer should not always stop"}, {"question": "Why does userspace matter?", "options": ["It gives normal apps a safe place to work", "It makes all apps disappear", "It turns the computer into a school bus", "It stops all helpers from working"], "answer": "It gives normal apps a safe place to work"}, {"question": "What does the kernel protect?", "options": ["Important computer parts", "Only lunch boxes", "Only shoes", "Only playground balls"], "answer": "Important computer parts"}, {"question": "Which sentence is easiest and correct?", "options": ["Userspace is where normal Linux apps work safely", "Userspace is where chairs run websites", "Userspace is only a toy box", "Userspace means apps cannot work"], "answer": "Userspace is where normal Linux apps work safely"}]}}}	1
